We need to address bug: With Live redirect_stdout enabled, sys.stdout becomes a FileProxy, which lacks fileno attribute. Subprocess expects fileno and raises error. The fix likely to make FileProxy implement fileno method delegating to underlying file (or using get_fileno). So modify FileProxy to add fileno method returning appropriate underlying fileno (or None?). Better to expose fileno attribute if underlying file has it.

Search if other parts need fileno. FileProxy implements __getattr__ that forwards attribute access to underlying file; thus fileno would be accessed via __getattr__, but only if attribute exists. However, fileno is a method on underlying file; __getattr__ returns getattr(self.__file, name). So calling sys.stdout.fileno() should work because FileProxy has __getattr__ and will forward to underlying file. However the error indicates that the FileProxy doesn't have fileno defined: maybe __getattr__ is not called for special methods? fileno is a special method looked up on the object itself, not via attribute access? In Python, calling .fileno() is attribute lookup like any method; __getattr__ should work. Why didn't it? Possibly because FileProxy inherits from io.TextIOBase, which defines fileno method that raises OSError (or not)? Let's check io.TextIOBase fileno implementation. TextIOBase doesn't implement fileno; calling .fileno on TextIOBase raises UnsupportedOperation? Wait, unbuffered text file? Actually TextIOBase (from io) defines fileno raising OSError. But because FileProxy inherits from io.TextIOBase and doesn't override fileno, attribute lookup finds fileno defined on parent class, so __getattr__ not invoked. So calling sys.stdout.fileno() uses the inherited method that raises OSError. To fix, need to override fileno to delegate to underlying file.

Thus modify FileProxy class, add method fileno(self) returning get_fileno(self.__file) maybe using _fileno utility. Should also implement isatty? but not needed.

